nd cross-loaded features in higher alpine terrain could have pockets of wind slab. Ice climbers and scramblers should be mindful of the consequences of even a small avalanche. Check out this link for some information on \u003ca target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" href=\"https://avalanche.ca/early-season-conditions\"\u003eEarly Season Conditions\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","confidence":{"rating":{"value":"low","display":"Low"}},"summaries":[{"type":{"value":"avalanche-summary","display":"Avalanche Summary"},"content":" \u003cp\u003eNo recent observations or reports.\u003c/p\u003e"},{"type":{"value":"snowpack-summary","display":"Snowpack Summary"},"content":" \u003cp\u003eField observations are very minimal. 20-30cm of snow can be found in higher Alpine areas. Isolated pockets of wind slab in lee and cross-loaded features are possible with slabs up to 50cm deep due to wind loading. The snowpack does not have a lot of layering structure at this time and any avalanche that initiates is likely to fail at the ground.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIce climbers should be thinking about overhead terrain and the influence of winds and warmer temperatures, potentially making triggering more likely.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eEven a small avalanche can have big consequences if a terrain trap, such as a cliff or confined gully, is part of the equation.\u003c/p\u003e"},{"type":{"value":"weather-summary","display":"Weather Summary"},"content":" \u003cp\u003eMore details can be found in the \u003ca href =\"https://www.avalanche.ca/weather/forecast\"\u003eMountain Weather Forecast\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e"}],"dangerRatings":[{"date":{"value":"2026-11-17T23:30:34Z","display":"Tuesday"},"ratings":{"alp":{"display":"Alpine","rating":{"value":"earlyseason","display":"Early Season"}},"tln":{"display":"Treeline","rating":{"value":"earlyseason","display":"Early Season"}},"btl":{"display":"Below Treeline","rating":{"value":"earlyseason","display":"Early Season"}}}},{"date